.TH DENIAL-SESSION 1 "August 2026" "Denial" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
denial-session \- start or validate an installed Denial desktop session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B denial-session
.RI [ \-\-check ]
.br
.B denial-session
.RI [ deniald-options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B denial-session
is the supported entry point for an installed Denial graphical session. It
resolves the display and optional render devices by capability, initializes
and validates the user's output configuration, locates the packaged Flutter
bundle, discovers an available user-session lifecycle, and starts
.BR deniald (1).
.PP
Display-manager session entries invoke this command directly. On systems with
an active systemd user manager, Denial activates its packaged
.B denial-session.target
after the compositor has published its endpoints and completed initial KMS
presentation. That target also activates systemd's
.B xdg-desktop-autostart.target
so standard XDG desktop autostart entries run only after Denial is ready. On
other logind-compatible systems the launcher process owns the compositor
lifecycle directly and Denial does not process XDG desktop autostart entries.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Validate the compositor, control client, session lifecycle, Flutter bundle,
output configuration, DRM selection, renderer, Qt platform theme, and Xwayland
without starting a compositor.
.TP
.B \-\-start-locked
Start with Denial's native authentication gate locked. Use this for direct or
autologin sessions that were not authenticated by a display manager.
.PP
All other arguments are forwarded to
.BR deniald (1).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.I /etc/denial/session.conf
Root-owned shell-compatible machine configuration. Supported overrides include
.BR DENIAL_DRM_DEVICE ,
.BR DENIAL_RENDER_DEVICE ,
.BR DENIA_SHELL_PROFILE ,
.BR DENIA_FLUTTER_RENDERER ,
.BR QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME ,
and
.BR DENIAL_FLUTTER_BUNDLE .
.PP
Unless explicitly overridden, the launcher selects Qt's
.B xdgdesktopportal
platform theme provider so Qt applications receive Denial's colour-scheme
preference from the Settings portal. Assigning an empty
.B QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME
value restores Qt's toolkit default.
.TP
.I /etc/denial/outputs.conf
System output template copied to a user's configuration on first launch.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/denial/outputs.conf
Per-user persistent output configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/denial/settings.json
Rust-owned per-user settings document. The Settings application submits
application-environment changes through Denial's native settings bridge;
deniald validates and atomically writes the document. String values in its
.B applicationEnvironment
default and per-application maps set variables and null values remove them.
Per-application maps are keyed by standard desktop-file IDs and override the
default map only when Denial launches that desktop entry. Native shortcuts
using the Application target carry the same identity; raw Program and Shell
command shortcuts receive only the default map. Changes apply to subsequent
launches without restarting Denial. These overrides do not affect systemd or
D-Bus activated applications, including XDG desktop autostart entries.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/autostart/*.desktop
Per-user XDG desktop autostart entries. These are processed by the systemd user
manager when available; use
.B OnlyShowIn=Denial;
to restrict an entry to Denial.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS/autostart/*.desktop
System and administrator XDG desktop autostart entries processed by the
systemd user manager.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/denial-session.target
Packaged graphical-session target used when a systemd user manager is
available.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates that validation succeeded or that the compositor exited
normally. Installation, configuration, device-discovery, and compositor
failures return nonzero.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR deniald (1),
.BR denialctl (1)
